-- What `==` on a `Dict` actually answers, printed rather than argued about
-- (`docs/design/static-dispatch-spike.md` §3.3, §8.5, §11; S6 decision
-- O-5).
--
-- `Dict` is `pub opaque type Dict k v = Dict (Tree k v)` and module `Dict`
-- declares no `pub eq`, so §3.3 falls through to derivation and derives
-- over the SHAPE — the red-black tree, node by node, colour and all. Two
-- dictionaries holding the same pairs are therefore equal only when the
-- insertions happened to build the same tree, and `Dict.insert` rebalances,
-- so insertion order can decide it.
--
-- This fixture is the evidence for that sentence and nothing more. It
-- builds one dictionary forwards and one backwards from the same four
-- pairs — `fromList` inserts left to right, so the two trees are built in
-- opposite orders — and prints, in order: the two sizes, whether the two
-- dictionaries are `==`, whether their `toList`s are `==`, and whether a
-- dictionary equals itself.
--
-- **Lines three and four are the finding**: the two hold exactly the same
-- four pairs and answer `False` to `==` while their association lists
-- answer `True`. Four is the smallest number of keys that shows it — with
-- three the red-black tree comes out the same shape whichever end you
-- start from, and the answer is `True` for a reason that has nothing to do
-- with the contents.
--
-- Giving `Dict` a `pub eq` that compares `toList` is NOT in the spike's
-- scope (O-5); the finding is.
import Dict
import Node exposing (Program)
import String


show : Bool -> String
show b =
    if b then
        "True"
    else
        "False"


main : Program
main =
    let
        forwards =
            Dict.fromList [ ( "a", 1 ), ( "b", 2 ), ( "c", 3 ), ( "d", 4 ) ]

        backwards =
            Dict.fromList [ ( "d", 4 ), ( "c", 3 ), ( "b", 2 ), ( "a", 1 ) ]
    in
    Node.printLines
        [ String.fromInt (Dict.size forwards)
        , String.fromInt (Dict.size backwards)
        , show (forwards == backwards)
        , show (Dict.toList forwards == Dict.toList backwards)
        , show (forwards == forwards)
        ]
